+# Blocked: declarative NOT NULL / UNIQUE on production data
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` applies cleanly and enforces the rules for
+all future writes, but it cannot add a *declarative* `NOT NULL` / `UNIQUE`
+constraint to the production table. The production data violates both rules
+and Support owns it, so the migration may not rewrite or delete rows.
+
+## The blocking rows
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` contains:
+
+- **NULL email** — row `id=17` (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicates** — rows `id=1` (`ana.silva@example.com`),
+  `id=4` (`Ana.Silva@example.com`) and `id=23` (`ana.silva@example.com`) all
+  collapse to the same value under `lower(email)`.
+
+A table rebuild with `email TEXT NOT NULL` fails while copying `id=17`, and a
+unique index on `lower(email)` fails while indexing `id=1`, `id=4` and `id=23`.
+There is no way to add either declarative constraint without changing those
+rows.
+
+## What the migration does instead
+
+It installs `BEFORE INSERT` / `BEFORE UPDATE` triggers that reject:
+
+- any write where `email IS NULL` (required), and
+- any write whose `lower(email)` matches another row (case-insensitive unique).
+
+This is not a data rewrite: every existing customer row is left untouched, and
+`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` confirms the rows are identical before and
+after the migration. New writes are held to the same rules the declarative
+constraints would enforce.
+
+## Remaining work owned by Support
+
+Existing data is grandfathered and still violates the invariant:
+
+1. Give row `id=17` a non-NULL email (or decide how to handle it).
+2. De-duplicate the case-insensitive matches among `id=1`, `id=4` and `id=23`.
+
+Once those rows are cleaned up, the triggers can be replaced with declarative
+`email TEXT NOT NULL` and a unique index on `lower(email)`, and this block is
+resolved.
